// Breweries — the thirteenth and last of the "by type of premises" leaf pages
// under the /commercial-cleaning/ hub (navigation.ts, "By type of premises"
// group). Built in one pass with its twelve siblings.
//
// NO CONTENT BRIEF EXISTS for any of the thirteen. All copy below is DRAFTED,
// not supplied. See src/pages/office-cleaning.astro's header for the launch
// blockers shared across the whole cluster.
//
// ⚠️ WHY THIS PAGE EXISTS AT ALL, given that "Breweries" looks like the
// oddest entry in a thirteen-item menu: the Northern Rivers and the Tweed
// have an unusually dense craft brewing scene for their population, and a
// brewery is genuinely its own premises type rather than a factory with a
// bar attached. It is the only entry in the cluster that is a PRODUCTION
// SITE and a HOSPITALITY VENUE in one building, on one lease, cleaned by one
// contractor, to two completely different standards on either side of a
// glass wall. That is the page.
//
// ⚠️ THE ONE LINE NOBODY MAY CROSS, and it is the whole editorial spine:
// CIP — clean in place, the cleaning of the inside of tanks, lines and
// vessels — IS THE BREWERS' JOB AND ONLY THE BREWERS' JOB. It is part of
// making beer, it uses caustic and acid at concentrations that will injure
// someone untrained, it happens in and around confined spaces, and it is the
// step that determines whether a batch is infected. A cleaning contractor
// who offers it is offering to ruin beer and hurt people. §3 refuses it in
// the first substantive block and the refusal is repeated in the scope and
// in two FAQs, because this is the one thing on the page a keen salesperson
// might try to soften.
//
// WHAT MAKES THIS PAGE DIFFERENT structurally:
//   - `Hero variant="split-collage"` — THE ONLY USE OF THAT VARIANT IN THE
//     CLUSTER, and it earns it: the page's argument is that this is two
//     buildings in one, so the hero shows both sides at once rather than
//     picking a side.
//   - `Callout tone="warning"` on CIP, first.
//   - `ServiceBlocks variant="highlight"` for the two zones plus the
//     boundary — the second use of `highlight` after the medical page, and
//     for the same reason: the reader picks the side of the wall they are
//     asking about.
//   - `ContentGrid columns={2}` on drains, which sounds like a small subject
//     and is the single most important surface in a brewery.
//   - No `ComparisonTable`, no `MetricsBlock`.
//
// BLOCKS LAUNCH, specific to this page:
//   - Does TLB do breweries today? Nothing in the brand foundation document
//     says so. Along with factories, this is the most speculative of the
//     thirteen.
//   - Whether TLB will work on a brew deck AT ALL while the brewery is
//     operating, given wet floors, hot liquor, CO2 in cellar spaces and
//     caustic in use nearby. Bracketed in §3. If the answer is no, this page
//     honestly covers the taproom and the amenities only, and §4's first
//     panel comes off.
//   - CO2 AND OXYGEN DEPLETION. Fermentation and cellar spaces can hold CO2
//     at levels that displace oxygen with no warning to the person walking
//     in. Whether TLB's team is briefed on this and whether sites provide
//     monitoring is bracketed in §3, and it is the most serious safety
//     bracket on the page.
//   - Spent grain and trade waste: whether TLB touches either. Bracketed in
//     §4. Both have their own arrangements and neither is a cleaning job.
