// §2 Definition. Answer-target block. Paragraph two draws the CIP line in the
// reader's own vocabulary before §3 refuses it formally, because a brewer
// who reaches §3 without this paragraph will read the refusal as ignorance
// rather than as discipline.
const definitionBody: TextBlockBody[] = [
  'Brewery cleaning is the cleaning of a brewery’s environment: brew deck and cellar floors, trench drains and floor waste, tank exteriors and the walls and skirtings around them, the keg and packaging area, plus the taproom, bar, bathrooms and back of house. TLB Cleaning does this work across the Northern Rivers and the Tweed for craft breweries, taprooms and cellar door venues.',
  'It stops at the outside of the vessel, and that is not a limitation we are apologising for. Cleaning in place — the caustic and acid cycles that clean the inside of tanks, lines and hoses — is part of making beer. It sits with your brewers, on your own schedule, with your own chemistry and your own verification, because it is the step that decides whether a batch is sound. Everything outside the vessel is a cleaning job, and there is a lot more of it than most brewery scopes account for.',
];

// §3 CIP and CO2. `warning`, and FIRST — before any scope.
//
// THE SPINE OF THE PAGE. See the file header: this is the one block a keen
// salesperson might try to soften, and it must not be softened.
//
// ⚠️ VERIFICATION PASS REQUIRED, and this block is as serious as the
// construction page's silica callout:
//   - CIP uses caustic (typically sodium hydroxide) and acid cycles at
//     concentrations and temperatures that cause serious chemical burns and
//     eye injury. Correct.
//   - Tank interiors are confined spaces; entry requires a permit,
//     atmospheric testing and specific training. Correct.
//   - Fermentation produces CO2 in large volumes; CO2 is heavier than air,
//     collects at low level and in cellars and tank pits, and displaces
//     oxygen with essentially no warning to the person entering. Correct,
//     and it is a documented cause of death in breweries and wineries.
//     NO PPM FIGURE IS QUOTED, on purpose — exposure standards move and a
//     wrong number in a safety warning is worse than none.
//   - Whether monitoring is present is a site question, which is why the
//     paragraph asks rather than asserts.
//   - SafeWork NSW and Safe Work Australia are the right primary sources and
//     the brand foundation document §3.3 already approves them.
const cipCallout = {
  heading: 'We do not touch the inside of a tank, a line or a hose. Ever.',
  body: [
    'Clean in place is brewing, not cleaning. The cycles run caustic and acid at concentrations and temperatures that cause serious burns and permanent eye injury, the vessels themselves are confined spaces that need a permit and atmospheric testing before anyone goes in, and the whole process is what determines whether your next batch is infected. It belongs to your brewers, with your chemistry and your verification records, and any cleaning contractor offering to take it over is offering to ruin your beer with the best of intentions.',
    'The second thing, and the one we would rather over-explain than under-explain: CO2. Fermentation produces it in volumes that matter, it is heavier than air, and it pools at floor level, in cellars and in tank pits with no smell and no warning — people have died walking into brewery spaces that looked completely ordinary. So our team is briefed to treat any low or enclosed space as unsafe until your people say otherwise, and to walk out rather than push on. [Whether TLB’s team is formally briefed on CO2 and oxygen depletion, and whether sites are asked to provide monitoring or a sign-off before entry. Confirm before this page ships.]',
  ],
};

