// §6 "Worth knowing". The lever here is unique in the cluster: on every other
// premises type poor cleaning costs money slowly. In a brewery it can cost a
// whole batch, and a brewer can price that themselves.
//
// ⚠️ VERIFICATION PASS REQUIRED:
//   - Beer spoilage organisms of concern include wild yeast (notably
//     Brettanomyces) and lactic acid bacteria (Lactobacillus, Pediococcus).
//     Correct.
//   - Drains, floors and hose ends are recognised environmental reservoirs
//     for these organisms in breweries. Correct in substance.
//   - Beer is a food for regulatory purposes in Australia and a brewery is a
//     food business, so premises cleanliness obligations under the Food
//     Standards Code apply. Correct in substance; no clause quoted.
//   - A taproom serving food has the same obligations as any other food
//     service premises. Correct.
//   - NO SPOILAGE STATISTIC OR DOLLAR FIGURE IS QUOTED, because any specific
//     number would need a source this page cannot cite, and the paragraph
//     asks the reader to do their own sum instead.
const worthKnowingBody: TextBlockBody[] = [
  'A brewery is the only premises type on this site where poor environmental cleaning can cost you an entire batch. The organisms that spoil beer — wild yeast, Lactobacillus, Pediococcus — do not usually arrive from outside. They live in the building: in drains, in floor corners, on the ends of hoses left on a wet floor, in the biofilm under a grate nobody lifts. Your CIP protects the inside of the vessel beautifully and does nothing at all about the room the vessel is standing in. Take a batch, multiply by your wholesale value, and that is the number the floor and the drains are actually protecting.',
  'The regulatory side is simpler than people expect and catches breweries out anyway. Beer is a food, which makes a brewery a food business, so the same obligation to keep premises, fixtures, fittings and equipment clean and sanitary applies to you as to a commercial kitchen — and if your taproom serves food, that side of the wall carries the full food service obligations too. Councils inspect both. The practical consequence is that your cleaning needs to exist as a written schedule with frequencies against each area, because that is the only form of it an inspector or a wholesale customer can actually look at.',
];

